# p_alloc_s8: diagnostic. s8 lever set with the allocator pinned.
#
# reason     p_alloc_s4/s5 were measured at a different operating point (peaks 2.1 to 2.3 GB, before the
#            per-layer driver, streaming loader and chunking levers); the line now ends at s8_encrypt_chunk,
#            881,272 kB.
# earlier
#              s4 at the line's operating point   2,289,520 kB   spread 9.35%    wall 105 s
#              s4 with the allocator pinned       2,027,692      spread 0.574%   wall 131 s
#                                                   -11.4%       16x tighter     +24%
#            mechanism: glibc dynamic mmap threshold (encrypt_spill_cpu frees 201 blocks, largest
#            178,151,424 B; race vs the peak; explicit tunables disable it).
# question   s8 replicates span 8.80%; peak inside an encoder layer's forward in all runs (885,372 /
#            898,152 / 845,840 kB; bracketing markers 336,000 to 340,000): ~545 MB transient per layer.
#            (a) mmap-threshold race: pinning narrows the spread and lowers the peak (as at s4);
#            (b) which layer holds the maximum: pinning changes variance, not level. The step file argues
#            (b) (29 HWM increases, largest 11%, last in layer 7; 91.8% before any graph node).
# scope      pinned allocator = different operating point (not publishable). Whether a runtime knob may be
#            a row is open (p_alloc_probe: never; runtime_knob: yes exception written for ARION a2_gogc50);
#            this probe supplies the number.
# levers     s8_encrypt_chunk.env's, same order (encrypt_chunk_cpu before encrypt_spill_cpu); only the
#            three malloc tunables added.
SHAFT_SCRIPT=$BASE/bert_perlayer_stream_cpu.py
SHAFT_LEVERS=plaintext_free_cpu,triple_share_free_cpu,trace_nograd_cpu,onnx_single_file_export_cpu,encrypt_chunk_cpu,encrypt_spill_cpu,embed_chunk_cpu
SHAFT_INNER_ENV="SHAFT_EMBED_CHUNKS=16 SHAFT_ENC_CHUNKS=8 MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=1 MALLOC_TRIM_THRESHOLD_=0 MALLOC_MMAP_THRESHOLD_=131072"
